<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-prime-factorization-activity">

	<title>Module 10: Prime Factorization &amp; Division Reprise</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Atoms</alert></p></introduction>
	


	<definition>
		<statement>
			<p>A <term>prime number</term> is an integer <m>p > 1</m> whose only positive divisors are 1 and <m>p</m>. A positive integer that is greater than 1 and not prime is said to be <term>composite</term>.</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</title>
		
	<theorem>
		<statement>
			<p>Every integer greater than 1 is either prime or a product of primes. Furthermore, this factorization is unique up to the order of the factors.</p>
		</statement>
	</theorem>
    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    	<p><em>Example:</em> We'll do a proof by (strong) induction. Let the statement <m>P(n)</m> be that <m>n</m> is either prime or a product of primes. Say that we are doing the inductive step and need to show that <m>P(6359) \to P(6360)</m>:</p>
    </exercise>
    
    <p>Let's prove it! We'll start with the proof of existence. Let <m>n \geq 2</m> and let the statement <m>P(n)</m> be that <m>n</m> is either prime or a product of primes.</p>
    
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    	<p>Prove the base case. (What is there to do, even??)</p>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
    	<introduction><p>
  For the inductive step, assume that for some integer <m>n\ge 2</m>,
  <m>P(2), P(3), \dots, P(n)</m> are all true.
  That is, suppose that for every integer <m>k</m> with <m>2\le k \le n</m>,
  <m>k</m> is either prime or a product of primes.
  (This is our <em>inductive hypothesis</em>.)
		</p></introduction>
		<task workspace="1in"><p>
  For the first case, suppose <m>n+1</m> is prime.
  Explain why <m>P(n+1)</m> is true in this case.
		</p></task>
		
		<task workspace="2.1in">
		<p>
  For the second case, suppose <m>n+1</m> is not prime.
  Explain why it is possible to find integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
  <m>n+1=xy</m> and <m>2\le x,y\le n</m>.
		</p>
		</task>
		
		<task workspace="2.1in">
		<p>
  What does the induction hypothesis allow you to conclude about the integers
  <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> from part (b)?
		</p>
		</task>
		
		<task workspace="2.2in">
		<p>
  Explain how your answers to parts (b) and (c) prove that <m>n+1</m> is a
  product of primes.
  Deduce that <m>P(n+1)</m> is true.
		</p>
		</task>
    </exercise>
    
    <p>There is a fill-in-the-blank proof near the end of Investigation 1 (p. 14-15) in your textbook.</p>
    </paragraphs>
 
 <page>
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Euclid's Lemma</title>
		
		<p>To prove the uniqueness part of the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic we will need to establish a result called Euclid's Lemma.</p>
		
		<lemma>
   		<title>Euclid's Lemma</title>

   		<statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers, and let <m>p</m> be a prime.
            If <m>p\mid ab</m> then <m>p\mid a</m> or <m>p\mid b</m>.
        </p>
  	  </statement>
		</lemma>
		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
		<p>
    Pick a non-prime number <m>k</m> and show that Euclid's Lemma is not true
    for <m>k</m>.
    (Show the negation of the conditional statement is true for some choice of
    integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.)
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
			<p>
    Explain why the following is a more general result.
    That is, why does Euclid's Lemma follow as a corollary from this theorem?
</p>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>a,b,</m> and <m>c</m> be integers.
            If <m>c\mid ab</m> and <m>\gcd(c,a) = 1</m> then <m>c\mid b</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise>
			<introduction><p>let's prove the theorem, and then get Euclid's Lemma as a corollary.</p></introduction>
			
			<task workspace="0.25in">
			<p>
    Use Bezout's identity to translate the assumption <m>\gcd(c,a) = 1</m> into
    an equation involving a linear combination.
			</p>

			</task>

			<task workspace="0.2in">
			<p>
    Multiply both sides of your equation from part (a) by an appropriate
    quantity in order to obtain an equation of the form
    <md>
        b = \underline{\hspace{.25in}}+ \underline{\hspace{.25in}}.
    </md>
			</p>
			</task>
			
			<task workspace="0.2in">
			<p>
    How can you use part (b) to conclude <m>c\mid b</m>?
			</p>
			</task>
		</exercise>
		
	</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
	<paragraphs>
	<title>Uniqueness proof</title>
		<p>Now we're ready to prove the <q>uniqueness</q> part of the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic. Recall:</p>
		
	<theorem>
    <title>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</title>

    <statement>
        <p>
            Every integer greater than <m>1</m> is either prime or a product of primes.
            Furthermore, this factorization is unique up to the order of the factors.
        </p>
    </statement>
	</theorem>
	
		<p>We'll use the strong form of Euclid's Lemma:</p>
		
	<lemma>
    <title>Euclid's Lemma - Strong Form</title>

    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>a_{1},a_{2},\dots, a_{n}</m> be integers and let <m>p</m> be
            a prime.
            If <m>p\mid a_{1}a_{2}\dots_{a}n</m>, then <m>p\mid a_{i}</m> for
            some <m>i</m> with <m>1\le i \le n</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
	</lemma>
	
	<exercise workspace="1in" xml:id="e-lem-start">
	<p>
    Let <m>n\ge 2</m> be an integer.
    As usual with uniqueness proofs, we will assume there are two possibilities
    for the prime factorization.
    Use <m>p_{i}</m> 's and <m>q_{i}</m> 's to describe how <m>n</m> is prime
    factored in two ways.
	</p>
	</exercise>

	<exercise workspace="0.75in" xml:id="e-lem-pt2">
<p>
    Explain why Euclid's Lemma - Strong Form implies that <m>p_{1}\mid q_{i}</m>
    for some <m>i</m>.
    Deduce that <m>p_{1} = q_{i}</m> for some <m>i</m>.
</p>
	</exercise>

	<exercise workspace="0.75in">
<p>
    Explain how your answer to <xref ref="e-lem-pt2" /> would allow you to cancel a factor from
    each side of the equation from <xref ref="e-lem-start" />.
    (Does multiplicative cancellation hold in the integers? Why?)
</p>
	</exercise>

	<exercise workspace="0.75in">
<p>
    Suppose in <xref ref="e-lem-start" />, you assumed <m>n</m> was the least integer that could be prime
    factored in <m>2</m> ways.
    Why now do you have a contradiction?
</p>
	</exercise>

	</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Primes in <m>\mathbb{E}</m></title>

<p>
    Recall that <m>\mathbb{E}</m>, the set of even integers, is a commutative ring without identity.
    We have the following definitions:
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                For <m>a,b\in \mathbb{E}</m>, we say that
                <term> <m>a</m> <m>\mathbb{E}</m>-divides <m>b</m> </term>
                provided that there exists <m>c\in \mathbb{E}</m> such that
                <m>ac = b</m>.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                A <term>prime number in <m>\mathbb{E}</m> </term> is a
                positive integer <m>p</m> that cannot be written as a product of
                two other integers in <m>\mathbb{E}</m>.
                That is <m>p\in\mathbb{E}</m> is prime provided that there do
                not exist even integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that
                <m>p=xy</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>

	<p>
    Let's see if the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic holds in
    <m>\mathbb{E}</m>.
</p>

	<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
    Give two examples and a non-example of pairs of integers <m>a</m> and
    <m>b</m> such that <m>a</m> <m>\mathbb{E}</m>-divides <m>b</m>.
    (Try to find an example where <m>a\mid b</m> in the integers, but
    <m>a\nmid b</m> in <m>\mathbb{E}</m>.
</p>
	</exercise>

	<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
    What are the first 10 prime numbers in <m>\mathbb{E}</m>?
</p>
	</exercise>
	
	<exercise workspace="0.35in">
<p>
    Find a way to write <m>60</m> as a product of primes in <m>\mathbb{E}</m>
</p>
	</exercise>
	
	<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
    Can you find another way to write 60 as a product of primes in
    <m>\mathbb{E}</m>?
</p>
	</exercise>

	<exercise workspace="0.35in">
<p>
    Is the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic true for <m>\mathbb{E}</m>? Explain.
</p>
	</exercise>

	<exercise workspace="0.35in">
<p>
    Is Euclid's Lemma true for <m>\mathbb{E}</m>? Explain.
</p>
	</exercise>
	</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Irreducible Polynomials</title>
		
		<p>
    In the integers, we have that Euclid's Lemma holds for prime integers.
    It turns out there's a difference between being prime and Euclid's Lemma
    holding in general rings.
    We have the following definitions, for an integral domain <m>R</m>:
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>r\in R</m> is nonzero and is not a unit.
                Then <m>r</m> is called <term>irreducible</term> in <m>R</m>
                if whenever <m>r=ab</m>, with <m>a,b\in R</m>, at least one of
                <m>a</m> or <m>b</m> must be a unit in <m>R</m>.
                Otherwise <m>r</m> is said to be <term>reducible</term>.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                The nonzero element <m>p\in R</m> is called <term>prime</term>
                if it is not a unit and whenever <m>p\mid ab</m> for any
                <m>a,b\in R</m>, then either <m>p\mid a</m> or <m>p\mid b</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>

<p>
    It turns out that prime elements are always irreducible, and in something
    called a <q>principal ideal domain</q> a nonzero element is prime if and only
    if it is irreducible.
    But prime and irreducible are not the same in general. To illustrate this point, let's think about irreducible polynomials.</p>

<definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
           
            For an integral domain <m>D</m>, a polynomial <m>f(x)</m> from
            <m>D[x]</m> that is neither the zero polynomial nor a unit in
            <m>D[x]</m> is said to be <term>irreducible over <m>D</m></term>
            if whenever <m>f(x)</m> is expressed as a product
            <m>f(x)=g(x)h(x)</m> with <m>g(x)</m> and <m>h(x)</m> from
            <m>D[x]</m>, then <m>g(x)</m> or <m>h(x)</m> is a unit in
            <m>D[x]</m>.
            A nonzero, nonunit element of <m>D[x]</m> that is not irreducible
            over <m>D</m> is called <term>reducible</term>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
    The following are examples of reducible and irreducible polynomials.
</p>

	<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Consider <m>f(x)=2x^{2}+4</m>.
    Is <m>f(x)</m> reducible or irreducible over <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>?
    <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>?
    <m>\mathbb{R}</m>?
    <m>\mathbb{C}</m>?
    (Hint: What are the units in these integral domains?
    What are the roots of the polynomial?)
</p>
	</exercise>
	
	<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Consider <m>g(x) = x^{2}+1</m>.
    Show <m>g(x)</m> is reducible over <m>\mathbb{Z}_{5}</m>.
    (Hint: Find roots.) Is <m>g(x)</m> reducible over <m>\mathbb{Z}_{3}</m>?
</p>
	</exercise>

	</paragraphs>
</page>

<page xml:id="sec-prime-factorization-review">

<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>
Make sure you can answer the following questions:

<ul>
	<li><p>How do we define a prime integer?</p></li>
	<li><p>What does the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic say?</p></li>
	<li><p>How do we prove the existence part of the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic? How do we prove the uniqueness part of the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic?</p></li>
	<li><p>What does Euclid's Lemma say? What is an example of Euclid's Lemma not working for non-prime integers? How can we generalize Euclid's Lemma to non primes? To more integers?</p></li>
	<li><p>What is the difference between being prime and irreducible?</p></li>
</ul>
                
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 10</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		<title>Non-unique factorization</title>
		<statement><p>
    Find an element of <m>\mathbb{E}</m> other than <m>60</m> that has more than
    one prime factorization in <m>\mathbb{E}</m>.
    <!--inv 4 #9-->
		</p></statement>
    	</exercise>
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<title>An irrational property</title>
		<statement><p>
    Recall that an irrational number is one that cannot be written as a ratio
    <m>\frac{a}{b}</m> for integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> and <m>b\neq 0</m>.
    Use Euclid's Lemma to prove that for all positive integers <m>n</m> and
    <m>q</m>, if <m>\sqrt[q]{n}</m> is not an integer then <m>\sqrt[q]{n}</m> is
    irrational.
    <!--Inv 4 #2-->
		</p></statement>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="3">
		<title>Reducible and irreducible polynomials</title>
		<introduction><p>
    Take the following theorem without proof (for now): Let <m>F</m> be a field.
    If <m>f(x)\in F[x]</m> and the degree of <m>f(x)</m> is <m>2</m> or
    <m>3</m>, then <m>f(x)</m> is reducible over <m>F</m> if and only if
    <m>f(x)</m> has a zero in <m>F</m>.
</p></introduction>
        	<task><p>
    Explain why <m>\mathbb{Z}_{5}</m>, <m>\mathbb{Z}_{3}</m>, and
    <m>\mathbb{Z}_{2}</m> are fields.
			</p></task>
			
			<task><p>
    Determine if the these polynomials are reducible or irreducible, by finding
    zeros and applying the theorem.
</p>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
    <m>f(x) = x^{3}+1</m> over <m>\mathbb{Z}_{5}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
    <m>f(x) = x^{3}+1</m> over <m>\mathbb{Z}_{3}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
    <m>f(x) = x^{3}+1</m> over <m>\mathbb{Z}_{2}</m>
</p>
</li></ul>
			</task>
            <task><p>
    (Side quest - you don't have to do this one!) Can you find polynomials
    <m>g(x)</m> and <m>h(x)</m> such that <m>f(x)=g(x)h(x)</m> for the fields
    for which <m>f(x)</m> is reducible?
			</p></task>
		</exercise>
		
	
	</paragraphs>
	
 </page>
 
 </worksheet>

